

8. Formal Theorem Section

Theorem 1 (Homomorphic Functional Equivalence)

Let ( D ) be a clinical dataset and ( \hat{D} = E(D) ) its zero‑knowledge homomorphic encryption under CKKS/BFV.Let ( f: D \rightarrow \mathbb{R} ) be any polynomially representable physiological function.

Statement:[ f(\hat{D}) = \widehat{f(D)} ]

Proof Sketch:Homomorphic encryption ensures that addition and multiplication in the ciphertext domain correspond to addition and multiplication in the plaintext domain. Since all physiological functions in MED‑ESE are polynomially representable, their evaluation under encryption preserves structure. Therefore, evaluating ( f ) on ciphertext yields the encrypted result of evaluating ( f ) on plaintext.[ \therefore f(\hat{D}) = \widehat{f(D)}. ]

Theorem 2 (Deterministic Physiological State Evolution)

Let each organ state machine be defined as[ \mathcal{O}_j = (S_j, T_j) ]with finite state set[ S_j = {\text{OK}, \text{STRAINED}, \text{OVERLOADED}, \text{REPAIRING}}. ]

Statement:For any encrypted glandular input ( g \in G ), the transition function ( T_j(s, g) ) is deterministic.

Proof Sketch:The transition function ( T_j ) is defined as a fixed homomorphic polynomial mapping. Homomorphic polynomials are deterministic functions: identical inputs produce identical outputs. Since ( G ) is encrypted but deterministic, and ( T_j ) is fixed, each pair ( (s, g) ) yields a unique next state.[ \therefore \mathcal{O}_j \text{ is deterministic}. ]

Theorem 3 (Continuity of Dynamic Engines)

Let the dynamic engines be defined as differentiable encrypted functions:[ \sigma(t), \rho(t), \epsilon(t), \mu(t) \in \hat{\mathbb{R}}. ]

Statement:Each engine is continuous over time:[ \frac{d}{dt} f_{\bullet}(t) \text{ exists and is continuous}. ]

Proof Sketch:Each engine is constructed from weighted sums of encrypted glandular signals and state‑machine characteristic functions. Glandular signals are polynomially encoded and therefore continuous. Characteristic functions change only at discrete state boundaries. Since transitions are discrete and finite, the engines are piecewise continuous with well‑defined derivatives between transitions.[ \therefore f_{\bullet}(t) \text{ is continuous}. ]

Theorem 4 (Ledger Integrity and Non‑Repudiation)

Let the transition ledger be defined as a sequence of blocks:[ B_k = (\widehat{S(t_k)}, \widehat{S(t_{k+1})}, \Pi_k). ]

Statement:If all proofs ( \Pi_k = \text{VALID} ), then the ledger is cryptographically non‑repudiable.

Proof Sketch:Each block contains a cryptographic proof verifying that the transition from ( S(t_k) ) to ( S(t_{k+1}) ) was produced by the global transition operator ( T ). If all proofs are valid, then every transition is authenticated. Since proofs are homomorphically bound to encrypted states, no adversary can alter a block without invalidating its proof.[ \therefore L \text{ is non‑repudiable}. ]

Theorem 5 (Identity‑Stable Evolution)

Let identity drift be defined as:[ \Delta I = I(t_2) - I(t_1) ]where ( I(t) ) is the encrypted identity‑drift timeline.

Statement:If the ledger is valid, identity drift is bounded and stable.

Proof Sketch:Identity drift is computed from mood and reward engines, both of which are continuous and deterministic. Since the ledger ensures that all transitions are valid, no unauthorized state mutation can occur. Therefore, identity drift evolves only through legitimate physiological transitions.[ \therefore \Delta I \text{ is stable under valid ledger evolution}. ]

Theorem 6 (Zero‑Knowledge Completeness)

Let ( E ) be the client‑side encryptor.

Statement:MED‑ESE satisfies zero‑knowledge completeness:[ \Pr[\text{Decrypt}(\hat{D}) = D] = 0 ]for any internal system component.

Proof Sketch:No decryption keys enter the system. No plaintext is stored. All computation is performed on ciphertext. Therefore, the probability of recovering plaintext from ciphertext without keys is negligible under standard homomorphic encryption assumptions.[ \therefore \text{zero‑knowledge completeness holds}. ]

Theorem 7 (Global System Determinism)

Let the global system state be:[ S(t) = (\sigma(t), \rho(t), \epsilon(t), \mu(t), \mathcal{O}(t)). ]

Statement:The global system evolution is deterministic.

Proof Sketch:Each component of ( S(t) ) is deterministic:

Glandular signals are deterministic polynomials.

State machines are deterministic (Theorem 2).

Dynamic engines are deterministic functions of deterministic inputs.

Ledger transitions enforce deterministic evolution.

Thus the entire system evolves deterministically.[ \therefore S(t) \text{ is deterministic}. ]

Theorem 8 (Encrypted Homeostasis Stability)

Homeostasis curves are defined as:[ H(t) = h(\sigma(t), \epsilon(t)). ]

Statement:Homeostasis curves remain stable under encrypted computation.

Proof Sketch:Stress and energy engines are continuous and deterministic. Their encrypted combination is also continuous. Since homomorphic encryption preserves functional structure, encrypted homeostasis curves behave identically to plaintext curves.[ \therefore H(t) \text{ is stable}. ]



